{"step_index":1412,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T22:09:20Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T06:09:20+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T06:09:24+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T06:09:20+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T06:09:24+08:00\nCommand exited with code 1.\n\nCommand output:\nbrain stats: { count: 6199, bytes: 56545875, nmCount: 5608, nmBytes: 41527677 }\nannotations\\8f2bd4b5-d81b-4839-aa40-a5c3a5acaa6a.pbtxt 92\nchapter_titles\\8f2bd4b5-d81b-4839-aa40-a5c3a5acaa6a.pb 181\nconversations\\8f2bd4b5-d81b-4839-aa40-a5c3a5acaa6a.db 26542080\nconversations\\8f2bd4b5-d81b-4839-aa40-a5c3a5acaa6a.db-shm 32768\nconversations\\8f2bd4b5-d81b-4839-aa40-a5c3a5acaa6a.db-wal 10067176\nS:\\ write probe:\nS:\\ root W_OK OK\n  File \"<string>\", line 7\r\n    for row in cur.execute(\" SELECT type name sql FROM sqlite_master WHERE sql IS NOT NULL\\):\r\n                           ^\r\nSyntaxError: unterminated string literal (detected at line 7)\r\n"}
{"step_index":1413,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T22:09:20Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T06:09:20+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T06:09:28+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T06:09:20+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T06:09:28+08:00\nThe search for \"Python sqlite3 connection.backup live WAL mode database atomic snapshot consistency\" returned the following summary:\nWhen working with SQLite in **WAL (Write-Ahead Logging) mode**, performing a naive file copy (like using `shutil.copy` on the `.db` file) is **unsafe and will result in data loss**[1]. In WAL mode, committed transactions often live solely in the separate `-wal` file until they are checkpointed back[1][2]. \n\nTo achieve an **atomic, consistent snapshot** of a live database without blocking readers or writers, Python provides the built-in SQLite Online Backup API via `connection.backup()`[3][4][5].\n\n---\n\n### Why `connection.backup()` Ensures Consistency in WAL Mode\n1. **Handles WAL Synchronization Automatically:** The underlying SQLite backup engine reads data from both the main database file and the active WAL file, ensuring that all committed states up to the start of the backup are safely transferred[1][5].\n2. **Atomic & Isolated:** The backup process creates a transaction-safe, point-in-time snapshot of the database into the target connection[3]. \n3. **Non-Blocking (Live Backup):** The source database remains fully accessible for reads and writes while the backup is running[3][6]. SQLite locks only pages as they are being read, minimizing contention.\n\n---\n\n### Code Example: Safe Live Backup in Python\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\nimport sys\n\ndef create_safe_backup(source_path: str, backup_path: str):\n    # Establish connection to the live source database\n    src_conn = sqlite3.connect(source_path)\n    \n    # Establish connection to the destination backup file\n    dest_conn = sqlite3.connect(backup_path)\n\n    try:\n        print(\"Initiating atomic online backup...\")\n        \n        # .backup() takes the destination connection.\n        # By default (pages=-1), it copies the entire database in one safe operation.\n        src_conn.backup(dest_conn)\n        \n        print(f\"Backup successfully written to {backup_path}\")\n        \n    except sqlite3.Error as e:\n        print(f\"An error occurred during backup: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        raise\n    finally:\n        # Clean up connections\n        dest_conn.close()\n        src_conn.close()\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    create_safe_backup(\"production.db\", \"backup_snapshot.db\")\n```\n\n---\n\n### Advanced: Progress Tracking for Large Databases\nIf you are backing up a massive database file, copying it all at once can temporarily hold locks or consume memory. You can use the `pages` parameter and a callback function to chunk the backup incrementally[3][6]:\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\n\ndef progress_status(status, remaining, total):\n    copied = total - remaining\n    print(f\"Progress: {copied}/{total} pages copied ({T_pct := (copied/total)*100:.1f}%)\")\n\ndef create_incremental_backup(source_path: str, backup_path: str):\n    with sqlite3.connect(source_path) as src_conn, sqlite3.connect(backup_path) as dest_conn:\n        \n        # Copy 100 pages at a time, invoking progress_status in between\n        src_conn.backup(\n            target=dest_conn, \n            pages=100, \n            progress=progress_status\n        )\n        \n    print(\"Incremental WAL backup completed successfully.\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    create_incremental_backup(\"production.db\", \"backup_snapshot.db\")\n```\n\n---\n\n### Alternative: `VACUUM INTO`\nIf you are running **SQLite 3.27.0 or newer**, you can also achieve a single-file atomic snapshot using SQL execution rather than the Python backup API[1][7]:\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\n\nwith sqlite3.connect(\"production.db\") as conn:\n    # Safely creates an independent, consistent snapshot file on disk\n    conn.execute(\"VACUUM INTO 'backup_snapshot.db';\")\n```\n*Note: `VACUUM INTO` also reads the WAL state properly and creates a clean, checkpointed single-file database clone[1][7].*\n\nSources:\n[1] [reddit.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFsXagQGvNXE-UAMIDgzYFdVpjtko2EWMEUF384JWQ6WAntme46ltKSgM5ZnMZHeiyCllZUDgDadgz9IrT71GbvEgES7B_g3F44D0nXuhyhohD1FB7K2iKoDrBr5CO18n88_Boz3UUxsuJn--d6vbwjZqHW3OCoAXqiRbh-V68NSfZv6K2tZxKgl55yXA32JTxy52QP)\n[2] [coddy.tech](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGIG-r75F10JoK_JulN9-vu45Nlas6DMNbPvP8FYdJZyTYdwvlQfN1qrLSqxNF8r63kVuCWACPu7KyJq2i0WWCSlCkOKW2feM8-t5I8njzCylDQc_gVC12xSSCref5LkM7sr5NtHnadIRmyn4GT)\n[3] [zetcode.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFnV-Ij2ULqIVS40NimWNmDcpa1vro3n0RqFT5SyVj97S4zfuTFw0vQU2YYjLj5tJ6f7K6maAQgFcvYRsR794sgBc8seuwEjYtfmF-4dYyFQC5CwqJJTY9DmMDoLsZ7EfXVEtyOahZ0ehaZXw==)\n[4] [kunalganglani.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHQK7aqbERBwtTyFj8GQ5t1pp2zjG6-VCcWtirzuCaHrl5Y1b_zTTMXg-0MpL7yzwmvGyY3CLRorEEPTPSf6kTd1prkuLkBDXsx8P5iPBS_qqYibyD3xGjPn6DGg8LU_7KxO9WNAVf0_qvRH66qseqHOPY=)\n[5] [coddy.tech](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEEC9j09wkQxdy3N40HWZJw7spA8consZ7q0E5tUaMZuOJFCsLGCXfm-ZLutX-ob2d6xnZUTDOV3o8Q-GKyr7qcBYWqEBIGO_KkE--6PVfJax9uOpxipzyzmEbuCa2NsTf9SlI5QOLA)\n[6] [python.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHfprmRyacNAKTg4YeiGAOxVh18bpz9XMdv0rcGJl65sE42ED-95YuYKcL8GJycu4JWOnhFsZdQlk-diDU80HRIcbfkOBB5r8Wf8AXUxJidtrHbF30-Ie9CmQqEWuG5Cr0HXFwp)\n[7] [codercops.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGc2AG1HC2hl_28KfcDrX4o9KyaBrSvWMSRqpgg80A5K4voaOWBYtER8XwULWkuWVmPofKM2O7qTRSaC2W4Y-i1-tepEl4TemZ47QlBFoUVYD_kdAId_RPWSbqIGnvsW6Udybsc04SxnmMNsoGEgrQbNgT3tcai2gCQc-NercwpjD-bu13qWx0-)\n\nNote: Source URLs are HTTP redirects; check where they resolve if you need the direct link."}
{"step_index":1414,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T22:09:21Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T06:09:21+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T06:09:33+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T06:09:21+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T06:09:33+08:00\nThe search for \"Antigravity IDE move or export conversation .gemini antigravity conversations db brain directory\" returned the following summary:\nIn the **Antigravity IDE** (and the broader Gemini Antigravity agent ecosystem), conversation history, context logs, and metadata are split across two primary layers: **human-readable brain artifacts/transcripts** and **internal SQLite/database indexes**[1][2]. \n\nDepending on whether you are trying to **move/migrate** your conversations between directories/versions (like upgrading to Antigravity IDE 2.0+) or **export** them into readable files, here is how the directory structure works and how to handle them.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Where Antigravity Stores Conversations & Brain Data\nAntigravity stores its operational data in two primary locations on your system (depending on your OS, typically Windows `%USERPROFILE%` / `%APPDATA%`[1] or macOS `~/.gemini/` and `~/Library/Application Support/`):\n\n* **The Brain / Artifact Directory (`~/.gemini/antigravity/brain/` or `~/.gemini/antigravity-ide/brain/`)**\n  * Contains folders named after individual conversation UUIDs[3][4].\n  * Inside each UUID folder, you will find raw execution logs (`transcript.jsonl`), scratch spaces, subagent trees, and generated files (`task.md`, implementation plans, code diff snapshots)[5][6].\n* **The Conversation Databases (`~/.gemini/antigravity/conversations/` or SQLite stores)**\n  * Contains `.db` files per conversation that track token metrics, model mappings, and transaction metadata[7].\n* **The IDE UI State Database (`%APPDATA%\\antigravity\\User\\globalStorage\\state.vscdb` or `antigravity-ide`)**\n  * An internal VS Code-style state database that maps what shows up in your sidebar history list (`trajectorySummaries`)[1][8].\n\n---\n\n### 2. How to Export Conversations\nBecause Antigravity heavily relies on internal database structures and JSONL transcripts rather than a simple user-facing export button[9], you have a few ways to export chats:\n\n* **Method A: Use an IDE Extension (Recommended)**\n  * You can install community-maintained utilities inside Antigravity (such as the *Antigravity History* extension from Open VSX)[10]. It adds a dedicated dashboard pane that lets you browse your local brain/db records and **one-click bulk export conversations into Markdown (`.md`) or JSON format**[10].\n* **Method B: Manual Extraction via the Brain Directory**\n  * If you navigate directly to your `brain/<conversation-id>/` folder, you can grab the `transcript.jsonl` file or any generated text artifacts (like architectural markdown plans)[6][2].\n  * *Tip:* You can feed a specific brain path directly back into a fresh session by prompting the agent: \n    > *\"Read `~/.gemini/antigravity/brain/<conversation-id>/` and summarize where we left off.\"*[3][2]\n\n---\n\n### 3. How to Move or Migrate Conversations (e.g., to Antigravity IDE 2.0)\nIf you are moving machines, updating versions, or your history list has gone blank after an upgrade, a simple file copy is usually required because the IDE state database needs to match the brain files[11].\n\n1. **Close the IDE completely** (make sure no background processes are running)[3].\n2. Locate your source data directories:\n   * **Brain & Conversation files:** `~/.gemini/antigravity/` (or `antigravity-ide`)[1][3]\n   * **UI State storage:** `%APPDATA%\\antigravity\\User\\globalStorage\\state.vscdb` (Windows) or `~/Library/Application Support/Antigravity/` (Mac)[1][12]\n3. **Perform the Copy / Merge:**\n   * Copy the contents of the old `brain/` and `conversations/` folders into your new environment's respective directories[13].\n   * If migrating between installations where the UI history l